WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.740
Good morning, Year 8. We have a navigation problem.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.450
Imagine a spaceship…

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.760
…with its directions stored. One wrong instruction could send…

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.170
…everyone the wrong way. Your job is to…

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:23.300
…read the route, find the right position and repair it using a Python list.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:30.820
Our WAGBA: getting better at using indexes to read and change values…

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.750
…in a Python list. Here is what that means.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.490
Knowledge: recognise a list and identify its elements.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.800
Remember that indexes always start at zero.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.690
In Python, a list starts with square brackets.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:50.830
Skills: create a list and learn how to print it.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.690
Create it, read one element…

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:58.810
…and replace a value using its index.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.130
Always remember: indexes start at zero.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:05.660
Understanding: explain what your code changed and what it left alone.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:11.180
Success is not just getting something onto the screen.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:17.750
Explain which position you changed, its index, and the output that proves it.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:24.120
Watch a small example. Let’s practise with colours before the spaceship’s route.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.730
Here is a list containing RED, BLUE and GREEN.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:31.460
BLUE is the second element, but its index is one.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.350
Python starts counting before most of us do: at zero.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:41.610
This print instruction reads index one, so the output is BLUE.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.090
Nothing has been removed or changed. Now replace that same…

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.250
…element with GOLD, and print the…

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.920
…whole list again: RED, GOLD and GREEN.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.690
Only the middle value changed. We still have three…

00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:02.570
…elements. That is the difference between reading and updating a value.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.190
In Main Task 1, create and read a route. In Main Task 2, repair it.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:14.020
Compare the task instructions with the expected output. Investigate and try again.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:21.610
Ready for more? The deeper tasks and Go Further challenges develop your skills.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:27.780
Type in the white Your Code area. Use square brackets to hold the list…

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.010
…and quotation marks…

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.680
…around text to make a string. Commas separate the elements.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:41.360
Press Run in the Python editor. Your result appears in the dark output area.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.500
Change your code? Run it again. During our live coding session…

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:52.670
…choose Open live question. You will have your own editor…

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.250
…and a countdown to work with.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:59.010
Try your code, press Run, then Send my response.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.210
Afterwards, we will compare ideas and improve together.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.870
I may award house points when you get it correct.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:10.970
